Q: Simple p5.js sound player, sound is forever &#39;undefined&#39; A: <p>Supplementing the <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/79957320/6243352">existing answer</a>, you can fix the problem immediately by pinning your p5.js to 1.11.3.</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-js lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>// this script is 100% unchanged from OP
let sound; 
let ply; 
function preload() { 

    // Initialize sound
    sound = loadSound("https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/8a/Computer_Sound_(Gravity_Sound).wav"); 
} 

function setup() { 

    //Checking playing or not
    var ply = sound.isPlaying();
    console.log(ply);

    playStopButton = createButton('Play');
    playStopButton.position(20, 20);
    playStopButton.mousePressed(playStopSound);
} 

function playStopSound() {
    sound.play();
    console.log(getAudioContext().state);
}</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;!-- old import:
&lt;script src="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/p5.js/2.0.5/p5.min.js"&gt;&lt;/script&gt; --&gt;

&lt;!-- new import: --&gt;
&lt;script src="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/p5.js/1.11.3/p5.min.js"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;
&lt;!-- the above 1.11.3 is the only thing that's changed from the original post --&gt;

&lt;script src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/p5.sound@0.2.0/dist/p5.sound.min.js"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>
<p>This is a <a href="https://q5js.substack.com/p/p5js-preload-system-removed-from" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><em>massive</em> breaking change</a>, and I'm shocked that p5 apparently hasn't communicated it well, since it invalidates every tutorial/code snippet with assets.</p>
<p>In spite of their elegance, promises are confusing for the beginner-level programmers often using p5 so I'm a bit surprised they've introduced them even disregarding fallout from the breaking change.</p>
<p>p5 docs still reference <code>preload</code> everywhere:</p>
<p><a href="https://i.sstatic.net/fzEY17i6.png" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><img src="https://i.sstatic.net/fzEY17i6.png" alt="screenshot of p5 docs showing preload and confusing ux" /></a></p>
<p>I haven't dove into p5.js 2.x yet further than this, but if it's anything like Python 2 to 3, or many other major breaking bumps, it could cause a ton of confusion. Pinning to 1.11.3 for the foreseeable future could avoid a number of other footguns (or perhaps this is the extent of it--I'm not sure, but it's a poor first experience). Once the beta period graduates, then you can upgrade.</p>
